[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Technical Tutorial: Relationship Modeling in Elasticsearch"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike relational databases, Elasticsearch is built on top of Apache Lucene, which does not natively support table joins at query time. Because Elasticsearch is designed for distributed, horizontal scaling, data is typically denormalized to ensure high query performance. However, real-world data is inherently relational. This tutorial explores the three primary strategies for handling relationships in Elasticsearch: Nested Objects, Join Fields, and Application-Level Denormalization."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Nested Data Type"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The nested data type is a specialized version of the object datatype that allows arrays of objects to be queried independently of each other. In a standard object array, Elasticsearch flattens object fields into a simple field-value structure, which causes relationship leakage. The nested type preserves the independence of each object inside the array."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"PUT /blogs
{
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "title": { "type": "text" },
      "comments": {
        "type": "nested",
        "properties": {
          "author": { "type": "keyword" },
          "message": { "type": "text" },
          "date": { "type": "date" }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"When querying nested documents, you must use a 'nested' query block to target the specific path."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"GET /blogs/_search
{
  "query": {
    "nested": {
      "path": "comments",
      "query": {
        "bool": {
          "must": [
            { "match": { "comments.author": "Alice" } },
            { "match": { "comments.message": "Great post" } }
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Join Data Type (Parent/Child Relationships)"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The join datatype acts as a parent/child relationship within a single index. This allows documents to be indexed independently while maintaining a hierarchy. Unlike nested objects, updating a child document does not require re-indexing the parent. However, this comes with a significant memory and CPU overhead during queries."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"PUT /company
{
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "my_join_field": {
        "type": "join",
        "relations": {
          "department": "employee"
        }
      },
      "name": { "type": "keyword" }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"To index a parent document, you specify the join field value matching the parent relation name. To index a child, you must provide the routing parameter pointing to the parent document ID."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"PUT /company/_doc/dept_1
{
  "name": "Engineering",
  "my_join_field": "department"
}

PUT /company/_doc/emp_1?routing=dept_1
{
  "name": "John Doe",
  "my_join_field": {
    "name": "employee",
    "parent": "dept_1"
  }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Denormalization (Application-Level Joining)"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Denormalization is the recommended approach for most high-performance Elasticsearch deployments. Instead of relying on Elasticsearch to resolve relationships at query time, you duplicate data across documents or store pre-joined documents during the ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) phase."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Extremely fast search queries because no runtime joining or nested block parsing is required."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Data redundancy and increased update complexity when a parent attribute changes."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary of Best Practices"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Denormalization when read performance is critical and child objects are updated infrequently."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Nested Objects when data is tightly bound (1-to-few relationship) and must be queried together safely within an array."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Join Fields (Parent/Child) only when 1-to-many or many-to-many relationships undergo frequent updates and denormalization overhead is too high."}]
